/**
@defgroup lib_chgame CHGame library
@brief The one include for a CHGame game: buttons, frame pacing, palette,
drawing, lettering, effects, sound, saving and the debug protocol.

@code
#include <CHGame.h>
@endcode

brings everything the twenty casino games share, on top of
@ref lib_chgfx "CHGfx" (the framebuffer, the panel and its DMA). It is modelled
on the Arduboy2 library: one global object, @ref ::chgame, with the same
button names, `nextFrame()` and `pollButtons()`, and a handful of small
namespaces for the rest (`pal::`, `fx::`, `audio::`, `save::`, `dbg::`).

Every CHGame game runs the same frame loop: logic, then `gfx_wait()` (the
previous frame has finished going out to the panel), then drawing into the
framebuffer, then `gfx_flushAsync()` (the panel gets it by DMA while the
next frame's logic runs).

@code
void setup() {
    chgame.boot();                    // buttons; START held 3 s goes back to the menu
    gfx_begin(GFX_DIV2, GFX_12BPP);   // the panel
    pal::init();                      // the house colours
    chgame.setFrameRate(60);
}

void loop() {
    dbg::poll();                      // the debug protocol (nothing in a release build)
    if (!chgame.nextFrame()) return;
    chgame.pollButtons();
    // ... logic ...
    pal::tick();
    audio::update();

    gfx_wait();                       // the previous frame has gone out: draw
    pal::commit();
    gfx_clear(FELT);
    // ... draw into the framebuffer ...
    gfx_flushAsync();                 // DMA to the panel while the next frame's logic runs
}
@endcode

The whole of that sketch, with a sound and a saved counter, is
@ref Hello.ino "the Hello example". The twenty games in the library's
`examples/Games/` folder are complete programs built on it.

@see [The library's README](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/platform/board/arduino/CHGame/libraries/CHGame/README.md),
[Getting started (for Arduboy developers)](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/docs/getting-started.md),
[Why the library is as it is](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/docs/chgame-library.md)
*/

/**
@defgroup lib_chgfx CHGfx library
@brief Wire-speed ST7735 graphics: a 16-colour framebuffer, DMA flushes,
sprites, shapes, text and fonts.

@code
#include <CHGfx.h>
@endcode

CHGfx drives the 128x128 ST7735S panel on SPI1 with DMA. It keeps a 4 bpp
(16-colour) framebuffer of 8 KB in SRAM and expands it through a palette to
the panel's 12 or 16 bpp while the DMA is already sending it, so a full
frame goes out at 90 fps (16 bpp) or 119 fps (12 bpp). @ref lib_chgame "CHGame"
is built on it, and a sketch that includes `CHGame.h` has all of CHGfx too.

Everything is a plain C function (`gfx_clear()`, `gfx_flushAsync()` ...);
the @ref CHGfx class and its one instance @ref Gfx are zero-cost forwarders
for those who prefer `Gfx.clear(0)`.

@code
gfx_begin(GFX_DIV2, GFX_12BPP);
gfx_setPaletteEntry(1, gfx_rgb(255, 255, 255));
gfx_clear(0);
gfx_text(4, 4, "Hello", 1);
gfx_flush();
@endcode

The rule that matters: drawing goes into the framebuffer, and the
framebuffer is free to draw into only after `gfx_wait()` (or
`gfx_waitRow()`), once the previous flush has finished reading it.

@see [The library's README](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/platform/board/arduino/CHGame/libraries/CHGfx/README.md)
(performance, the simulator, configuration and what everything costs),
[FONTS.md](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/platform/board/arduino/CHGame/libraries/CHGfx/FONTS.md)
*/

/**
@defgroup lib_chsd CHSd library
@brief Read-only microSD: an SPI-mode block driver and a FAT16/FAT32 reader
that turns a file name into runs of card blocks.

@code
#include <SdSpi.h>
#include <Fat.h>
@endcode

About 1.7 KB of flash and 24 B of RAM. The card shares SPI1 with the panel,
so every call goes between `gfx_wait()` and the next flush, and every call
borrows a 512-byte, 4-byte-aligned buffer (CHGfx's `gfx_chunkScratch()` is
idle at exactly that time).

@code
// Once, after gfx_wait():
fat::Run run[4];
uint8_t nRuns = fat::open("WORDS   DIC", run, 4, gfx_chunkScratch());
// ... then any block of the file, any time after gfx_wait():
if (!fat::read(run, nRuns, k, gfx_chunkScratch())) { /* the card has gone */ }
@endcode

Cards must be FAT16 or FAT32 (how cards up to 32 GB come); exFAT is
reported as fat::E_EXFAT so a game can ask for a reformat.

@see [The library's README](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/platform/board/arduino/CHGame/libraries/CHSd/README.md),
[SD card notes](https://github.com/bateske/CHGame/blob/main/docs/sd-card.md)
*/

/**
@defgroup lib_core Core libraries
@brief The Arduino libraries the board package's core provides: SPI, Wire
(I2C) and EEPROM, ported to WCH's chips.

These are the standard Arduino APIs, so sketches and libraries written for
other boards work unchanged. On the CHGame board, note:

- **SPI1 belongs to the panel and the SD card.** CHGfx drives it directly
  with DMA; use the @ref core_spi "SPI" class only for your own devices, and
  only between `gfx_wait()` and the next flush. The SPI class resets SPI1
  and sets its own clock and mode, so hand SPI1 back to CHGfx afterwards
  with `gfx_setSpiDiv(gfx_spiDiv())`.
- **Wire** (I2C) is on the expansion header: SDA PC18, SCL PC19, with no
  pull-ups on the board.
- **Saving a game** is done with the @ref chgame_save "save::" functions of
  the CHGame library, which keep a record in flash with a CRC and survive
  re-uploads. @ref core_eeprom "EEPROM" is the CH32V003's: on this chip its
  `commit()` writes nothing and returns `false` (rewriting the option bytes
  its way could leave the chip read-protected), and including it prints a
  compiler message.
*/

